Title: LOW TIDE RADIO
Credit: Written by
Author: OpenFrame evaluation fixture
Source: Low Tide Radio, a fictional film project
Draft date: 2027-01-10
Notes: Every person and place in this script is invented.

EXT. KADALPURAM FISH LANDING CENTRE - DAWN

Low tide. The sea has drawn back from the landing and left the rusted foot of the old radio mast standing in wet sand. Boats nose in one by one. MARIAMMA (60s, faded blue mundu, quick hands) sorts sardines into baskets without looking at them.

MARIAMMA
Jose, your boat is late again. The tide does not wait for your mother's prayers.

JOSE
The engine coughed twice, Mariamma chechi. Twice.

HARBOUR CLERK
Late boats go on the late list. Sign here.

JOSE
Sign it yourself. My hands smell of diesel.

INT. CHAAYA TEA SHOP - MORNING

Steam from a copper kettle. A transistor on the shelf hisses between stations. ROSILY (50s, flour on her forearms) pours tea into steel glasses.

ROSILY
Babu is buying the station land. Everyone knows. Even the crows know.

BABU
Land is not a feeling, Rosily. It has a price.

NANDAN
The station is not for sale. Sudheesh chettan says it still has a heartbeat.

BABU
A twelve year old and his heartbeat. Drink your tea.

EXT. BACKWATER FERRY JETTY - DAY

A green ferry bumps against the jetty. ANJALI (29, grey cotton kurta, canvas shoes, a leather case of cassette tapes under her arm) steps onto planks slick with last night's rain.

HARBOUR CLERK
Name, purpose, how long?

ANJALI
Anjali. Family. As long as it takes.

HARBOUR CLERK
Varghese's daughter?

ANJALI
Yes. Is the station gate still locked?

HARBOUR CLERK
Since 1999. Nobody has the key except the church.

INT. MARIAMMA'S HOUSE - DAY

A tiled house with a brass lamp in the corner. Mariamma stands in the doorway and does not step aside.

MARIAMMA
Twenty years of no letters, and now a suitcase.

ANJALI
You never answered mine.

MARIAMMA
I answered. Your mother's people returned them unopened.

ANJALI
Then we were both writing to a locked gate.

MARIAMMA
Your father's room is at the back. Wipe your feet.

INT. MARIAMMA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

Rain on the tiles. A biscuit tin with wires coming out of it sits between two glasses of water.

MARIAMMA
He built it from a biscuit tin and a borrowed valve. It never played anything clearly.

ANJALI
It played his voice.

MARIAMMA
That was the wind in the wire.

ANJALI
Aunty, what happened to him?

MARIAMMA
The sea happened. Eat your rice.

EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - DAY

A low concrete building under a tall steel mast. Bougainvillea has swallowed half the fence. A padlock the size of a fist hangs on the gate.

ANJALI
Is anyone allowed in?

NANDAN
Only the Father has the key. But at night the wires sing. I can hear them from my window.

ANJALI
What do they sing?

NANDAN
Numbers. Always numbers.

INT. ST. ANTONY'S CHURCH OFFICE - DAY

A cramped office smelling of candle wax. FR. THOMAS (70, white cassock, spectacles on a string) stirs sugar into a glass of tea he does not drink.

FR. THOMAS
Your father gave me this key the week before he sailed. He said, give it to my daughter when she asks.

ANJALI
I am asking.

FR. THOMAS
Twenty years is a long time to wait to be asked.

ANJALI
What is the condition?

FR. THOMAS
Whatever you hear on that radio, you tell your aunt first.

INT. HARBOUR OFFICE - DAY

A ceiling fan turns slowly over stacks of stamped paper. The Harbour Clerk lines up a rubber stamp with a ruler.

HARBOUR CLERK
The lease on the station land ends on the thirty-first. Renewal needs a licence.

ANJALI
What licence?

HARBOUR CLERK
A working one. Which needs a working station. Which needs a miracle.

BABU
Or a buyer. I have the cheque ready.

EXT. BABU'S BOAT YARD - DAY

Half-built hulls on wooden stocks. Babu leans on the bonnet of a pickup truck with an envelope in his hand.

BABU
Four million for a building that earns nothing. Your father would call it a blessing.

ANJALI
My father would call it a trap.

JOSE
Do not sell, Anjali kutty. Not to him.

BABU
Jose, the nets are drying. Go and watch them dry.

INT. CHAAYA TEA SHOP - NIGHT

One bulb over the counter. Moths. A plate of banana fritters between three glasses.

ROSILY
Sudheesh fixed every radio in Kadalpuram for thirty years. He is also the only man who cannot be bribed with fish.

SUDHEESH
I can be bribed with fritters.

ANJALI
Then I will bring fritters. Will you look at the transmitter?

SUDHEESH
I looked at it every night for twenty years. Through the fence.

INT. COAST RADIO STATION - NIGHT

The gate groans. A torch beam finds a desk, a dead microphone and a single cassette tape.

SUDHEESH
Mind the floor. Pigeons have been paying no rent.

ANJU
Whose cassette is this on the desk?

SUDHEESH
Not mine. Nobody has been in here since the night he sailed.

NANDAN
Can we switch something on?

SUDHEESH
Not yet. First we find out if the building will fall on us.

INT. COAST RADIO STATION - TRANSMITTER ROOM - NIGHT

Racks of glass valves. Sudheesh wipes each one with a soft cloth before he seats it.

SUDHEESH
Valves are glass. Glass remembers heat. Be gentle.

ANJU
It is humming.

SUDHEESH
That is not a hum. That is a long breath after a long sleep.

ANJU
Can it reach the boats?

SUDHEESH
Ten miles on a good night. Twenty if God is listening.

EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - NIGHT

The mast against a black sky. One red lamp blinks, then a second.

NANDAN
Amma, the lights are on! The mast is awake!

SALIM (V.O.)
Coast station, coast station, is this a test or a ghost?

NANDAN
It is Sudheesh chettan and a lady from the city!

SALIM (V.O.)
Then tell the lady the ghost says welcome.

INT. ANJALI'S RENTED ROOM - NIGHT

A bare room above the co-operative bank. A single bulb. Anjali threads a cassette into a small recorder and presses play. Hiss, then a man's voice, almost lost.

VARGHESE (V.O.)
Coast station, if you hear this, tell Mariamma the...

ANJALI
Baba?

EXT. KADALPURAM BEACH - MORNING

Wet sand, small waves. Nandan runs with a pocket radio held over his head.

NANDU
It works, Jose chetta! The station works!

JOSE
A station that works is trouble for somebody.

NANDU
For who?

JOSE
For whoever profits when it does not.

INT. GOVERNMENT DISPENSARY - DAY

A whitewashed room, a high window with no curtains, a metal bed with Mariamma seated at its edge.

DR. LEELA
Your aunt's chest is clear. Her silence is what hurts.

ANJALI
She will not speak about my father.

DR. LEELA
She watched his boat go down from this very window. It has no curtains for a reason.

MARIAMMA
Leela, you talk too much for a woman with a stethoscope.

EXT. KADALPURAM FISH LANDING CENTRE - DAY

Midday glare. A crowd around crates of ice and silver fish. A stray dog threads between legs.

BABU
Everything on her table, at double. Then let her sell air.

MARIAMMA
Then I will sell air, Babu. It is cleaner than your money.

JOSE
Half of it is mine. Triple, if you like.

HARBOUR CLERK
Order, order. Bid in the book, not in the air.

INT. COAST RADIO STATION - DAY

Daylight through a broken louvre. A coil of cable on the floor, one end cleanly sliced.

SUDHI
Cable is cut here. Cut, not worn. See the clean edge?

ANJALI
Someone cut it on purpose.

NANDAN
Somebody climbs that ladder at night. I told nobody.

EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - ROOF - DAY

A flat concrete roof, the mast bolted to its centre. The whole bay spreads out below, bright and unforgiving.

SUDHEESH
Do not look at the sea. Look at the bolt.

ANJALI
I am looking at the sea.

SUDHEESH
Then at least hold the rope.

INT. CHAAYA TEA SHOP - EVENING

Lamps just lit. Rosily wipes the counter. A tall man in a skull cap ducks under the doorframe.

ROSY
Salim bhai! Sit. The ghost has been asking for you.

SALIM
I have been talking to a ghost for a week. I came to see its face.

SUDHEESH
It has a screwdriver and a bad back.

SALIM
Then it is a real station.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE ROAD - DUSK

A road of crushed shell beside a white lighthouse. Anjali and Salim walk slowly. Far off a temple bell.

SALIM
I was twenty-two. He called every night at nine. Weather first, then jokes.

ANJALI
And the last night?

SALIM
No jokes. Only your name, three times, and then the sea.

INT. ST. ANTONY'S CHURCH - NIGHT

Candles along the nave. A hymn fades. Mariamma kneels on the same worn stone as always.

ACHAN
Mariamma, you have knelt on this stone for twenty years and asked for the same forgiveness.

MARIAMMA
Some forgiveness is not given in a church.

ROSILY
Then give it at home, Mariamma. The girl is under your roof.

INT. MARIAMMA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

The brass lamp throws a small circle of light. Anjali sets the recorder on the table.

ANJALI
Listen. He says your name.

AMMACHI
Turn it off.

ANJALI
Aunty, he says tell Mariamma the...

AMMACHI
Turn it off, Anjali, or leave my house.

EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - DAY

A jeep stops at the gate. The Harbour Clerk unfolds a notice and reads it to the whole street.

HARBOUR CLERK
Notice: unlicensed transmission is an offence. Fine, ten thousand rupees.

BABU
Pay it, or leave it. Either way the land comes to me.

ANJALI
We are testing, not transmitting.

HARBOUR CLERK
The mast is lit, madam. Testing is a kind of transmitting.

INT. HARBOUR OFFICE - DAY

The same ceiling fan. The Clerk looks at the door before he speaks.

ANJALI
I want a temporary experimental licence.

HARBOUR CLERK
There is no such thing.

ANJALI
Then there is a form that looks like one.

HARBOUR CLERK
Fill it in pencil. Pencil can be argued with.

INT. COAST RADIO STATION - NIGHT

The microphone on its stand, a red ON AIR bulb made from a torch lens. Anjali nods to Nandan.

ANJALI
This is Low Tide Radio, from the coast station at Kadalpuram. Tide table next.

NANDU
High water at two-forty. Low water at nine-ten. Wind from the south-west, gentle.

SUDHEESH
Voice a bit lower, Nandu. You are not selling fish.

SALIM (V.O.)
Coast station, this is Salim. You are loud and clear.

EXT. KADALPURAM BEACH - NIGHT

Forty small radios held up to the dark like candles. Surf behind them.

ROSILY
Quiet! It is the tide table!

JOSE
Twenty years I have guessed the tide with my knees.

MARIAMMA
Turn it up. No. Down. No, leave it.

NANDAN
That is me! I am the tide table!

INT. CHAAYA TEA SHOP - MORNING

The shelf transistor is off. Babu stands where the morning sun hits the counter.

BABU
The shop stands on my brother-in-law's plot. Ask him how long he will wait.

ROSILY
Waiting is what this village does best.

JOSE
Then we will wait louder.

INT. BABU'S BOAT YARD OFFICE - DAY

A tin-roofed office. A wall map of the coast with the station circled in red.

BABU
The substation switch is in your department, no?

HARBOUR CLERK
A power cut is a routine matter. It can happen on any night.

BABU
Make it happen on the right one.

EXT. BACKWATER FERRY JETTY - DAY

The same green ferry, the same slick planks. Salim holds a plastic-wrapped notebook against his chest.

SALIM BHAI
I kept this twenty years. My own log. Your father's last call is on page nine.

ANJALI
Why keep it from me?

SALIM BHAI
I kept it from the sea. The sea takes written things.

INT. COAST RADIO STATION - NIGHT

The cassette goes into the old deck. The reels turn. Static, then the sea.

VARGHESE (V.O.)
Coast station, Little Flower. The engine is gone. Jose is bailing. Tell Mariamma the net was not her fault.

ANJALI
The net? What net?

SUDHEESH
Play it again.

INT. MARIAMMA'S HOUSE - DAY

Morning light on the tiles. Mariamma has laid a tin box of old receipts on the table before Anjali arrives.

ANJALI
The net was not your fault. He said so.

AMMACHI
He said it because he was kind. I told him the monsoon would pass. It did not.

ANJALI
You have carried this twenty years.

AMMACHI
And you will carry it with me. That is what family is.

EXT. KADALPURAM BEACH - DAY (1999)

The same beach, a younger sky. YOUNG ANJALI (9, school pinafore, two braids) kneels at the waterline and launches a paper boat.

YOUNG ANJALI
Baba said he would be back before my boat reaches the rocks.

MARIAMMA
Then do not push it so fast, Kunju.

YOUNG ANJALI
It is the wind pushing it, not me.

INT. COAST RADIO STATION - NIGHT (1999)

The same room, thirty years younger in its paint. SALIM (22, checked shirt) wears headphones. Young Anjali sits on a stool, feet not touching the floor.

SALIM
Little Flower, this is the coast station. Say again, say again.

VARKEY (V.O.)
Salim, tell the little one to go to bed.

YOUNG ANJALI
Baba! I am not sleepy!

VARKEY (V.O.)
Then sing me the tide song, kutty.

EXT. OPEN SEA - LITTLE FLOWER - NIGHT (1999)

Black water, white spray. The Little Flower climbs a wave and drops. A hurricane lantern swings from the mast.

VARGHESE
Bail, Jose! The sea is not asking for permission!

JOSE
The net is dragging us! Cut it, Varghese chetta!

VARGHESE
If I cut it, Mariamma's whole year goes with it.

JOSE
Her year, or our lives?

VARGHESE
Tell me the tide song, Jose. Loudly.

INT. MARIAMMA'S HOUSE - NIGHT (1999)

Lantern light. The biscuit-tin radio hisses. Young Anjali is curled on the floor beside it.

YOUNG ANJALI
Aunty, why did Baba stop singing?

MARIAMMA
Radios sleep too, Kunju. Close your eyes.

EXT. KADALPURAM FISH LANDING CENTRE - DAWN

Dawn again at the landing. Jose mends a torn net with fingers that know the knots without him.

JOSE
I cut it, Anjali. I cut it and he said go, go, and I was a coward.

ANJALI
You came back.

JOSE
With half a boat and no brother. I have been late every day since.

INT. ST. ANTONY'S CHURCH - DAY

Sun through the side door. Fr. Thomas lays a leather log book on a pew.

FR. THOMAS
The log shows the cable was cut two nights before the storm. Someone wanted the station silent.

ANJALI
Babu's father had the boat yard then.

FR. THOMAS
I am a priest. I only read the log.

INT. COAST RADIO STATION - DAY

Every light dies at once. The valves glow orange in the dark like angry eyes.

BABU
A station without current is just a shed.

SUDHEESH
Nandan, the valves! Slowly off, slowly!

NANDAN
They are glowing like angry eyes!

EXT. KADALPURAM BEACH - DUSK

A crowd on the sand at dusk. Rosily stands on an upturned boat.

DR. LEELA
The lease needs a licence. The licence needs a signal. The signal needs power.

ROSILY
Then we bring power. Every house brings a battery.

MARIAMMA
And I bring the net money I owe the sea.

JOSE
My boat engine makes electricity if you shout at it.

EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - ROOF - NIGHT

Rain. A generator coughs under a tarpaulin. Nandan holds a lamp above the microphone.

ANJALI
Sudheesh, now!

SUDHEESH
Batteries are singing. Say your words.

ANJALI
This is Low Tide Radio. If you can hear us, answer with a light.

NANDAN
Lamps! Look at the boats, they are lighting lamps!

EXT. COAST RADIO STATION - DAY

Morning haze. Fifty villagers on the road outside the gate, tea flasks passed hand to hand.

MARIAMMA
Twenty years I looked away from this mast. Today I will look at it as long as I like.

BABU
The station is not licensed.

ROSILY
The village is. Count the lamps from last night.

ANJALI
Babu, the cheque is still in your hand.

INT. HARBOUR OFFICE - DAY

The ceiling fan. The Clerk lifts the stamp very slowly and brings it down.

HARBOUR CLERK
Experimental licence, valid one year. Fee, one rupee.

BABU
One rupee? That is an insult to the department.

HARBOUR CLERK
The department is very small, sir.

ANJALI
Here is your rupee.

INT. CHAAYA TEA SHOP - EVENING

Garlands of jasmine over the counter. The transistor is turned all the way up.

ROSILY
Tea is free tonight. Tomorrow it is double.

NANDAN
First request: a song for the fisherman who is always late.

JOSE
That is slander.

SUDHEESH
It is also the tide table.

EXT. KADALPURAM BEACH - LOW TIDE - DAWN

Low tide again. The rusted foot of the mast stands in wet sand. Gulls. Anjali and Mariamma stand shoulder to shoulder.

ANJALI
Aunty, he used to say the sea gives back at low tide.

MARIAMMA
What did he say it gives back?

ANJALI
Everything but time.

SALIM (V.O.)
Low Tide Radio, Salim here. Every boat on the coast heard you. Good morning, Kadalpuram.

